To make the code thread-safe, you can use `std::localtime_r` instead of `std::localtime_s`. `std::localtime_r` is a reentrant version of `std::localtime` that takes an additional pointer to a tm structure where the result is stored. This allows you to avoid the use of a static buffer and make the function thread-safe.

Here is an example of how you can modify the code to use `std::localtime_r`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <ctime>

int main() {
    std::string msg = "Test message";
    time_t now = time(nullptr);
    struct tm result;
    localtime_r(&now, &result);
    char buf[20];
    strftime(buf, sizeof(buf), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S", &result);
    std::cout << "[" << buf << "] Received: " << msg << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

By using `std::localtime_r` and providing a separate tm structure for each thread, you can ensure that the code is thread-safe and avoid potential portability issues related to the use of `std::localtime_s`.
